          "params": [
            {
              "name": "members",
              "type": "T",
              "optional": false
            }
          ],
          "returnType": "ObservableStruct<T>",
          "description": "Builds a frozen record with one observable per member of members: ts const player = Observable.struct({ name: 'Hero', health: 100, stats: Observable.struct({ ammo: 30, level: 1 }), }); player.health.value -= 10; player.stats.ammo.value--;  Each own enumerable member, string or symbol keyed, becomes an Observable holding that value; special names such as __proto__ are ordinary members. Members that already are observables (including derived values, vectors and sizes) or structs are kept as they are, by identity, so several structs can share them. Nothing is traversed: a nested object or array is stored as the value of one observable; nest structs explicitly instead. members itself is not modified. A struct owns nothing and has no teardown. The observables it created belong to whoever holds the struct and need no cleanup beyond dropping it; shared observables stay owned by their creator, and destroying one affects every struct that holds it."
        }
